HouseMenu.Rebuild accepts a floorplan basename. Build House keeps its default floorplan; the two added Build House menu entries select the ranches. Selection survives an assembly reload during the editor session. Returning to Build House replaces variant furniture as well as geometry. RecorderMenu has the requested brain-only menu names and run IDs unity_family_ranch_full and unity_long_ranch_full. Each run rebuilds its selected house and furniture before people are placed. The recording, fire, fluid smoke and oracle floorplan_used.json now all load that scenario's selected plan. The recorder copies floorplan_used.json before the first frame and refreshes it at checkpoints. Default-house restoration runs when a variant recording finishes or its iterator fails. A running recorder blocks a house rebuild.
RanchExteriorBuilder generates a 22-degree pitched roof, 0.45 m eaves, fascia, gables, white siding cut around the openings, exterior sills, a flush porch slab and canopy, driveway, four lawn strips, shrubs, a tree and a mailbox. HouseBuilder supplies the framed glass windows. All materials already exist in Art/Materials. The exterior uses Unity primitives and generated gable meshes. Ground-level decoration stays outside the footprint and has no colliders. The original ground slab supports the robots. Roof parts sit above the ceilings on the cutaway layer. This preserves the evaluator's house-floor boundary. I did not open, launch or control Unity, import packages, download assets, or record a live run.
The small validator checks room tiling, grid alignment, nonoverlap, the single entrance, doorway boundaries and widths, graph reachability, wall and furniture clearance, 0.9 m paths from each door to its room centre, people clear of furniture and doors, distinct survivor rooms, valid fire fuel, mission settings and numeric reasons. The furniture placer uses rectangular approach checks and the existing 0.1 m flood fill. The random generator's enclosing-circle approximation rejects ordinary beds in these smaller rooms, so it is not the acceptance rule here. Existing random-layout generation code remains unchanged.
Four new C# tests exercise actual Scenario and FireSim loading, inheritance without early survivor placement, rejection of paths outside config and the default-house fallback. Those tests caught sibling smoke-density reason strings being parsed as room floats. Scenario.Load now excludes only the density map's reason annotations before typed deserialization. The file retains its annotations.
family_ranch final headless result entered 9 of 9 spaces. Camera-searched usable floor was 97.85%, 11990 of 12253 samples. Gross room-floor coverage was 96.88%. The run confirmed 5 of 5 people and had 0 unmatched confirmation events. Both robots completed the walk out. robot_A ended in state 'complete' and was outside the house. It first logged a completed outside pose at 525.5 s. robot_B ended in state 'complete' and was outside the house. It first logged a completed outside pose at 508.5 s. 
long_ranch final headless result entered 11 of 11 spaces. Camera-searched usable floor was 95.65%, 13715 of 14339 samples. Gross room-floor coverage was 94.91%. The run confirmed 5 of 5 people and had 0 unmatched confirmation events. Both robots completed the walk out. robot_A ended in state 'complete' and was outside the house. It first logged a completed outside pose at 514.0 s. robot_B ended in state 'complete' and was outside the house. It first logged a completed outside pose at 504.5 s. 
These are headless measurements. The runner uses the unchanged brain with ray-cast sensors, a body capsule against walls and the other robot, fire-driven air temperature, the scenario's radio range and wall limit, and its 480-second deadline. Its person detector is idealized and does not model RGB errors, thermal images or smoke occlusion. Runtime collapse debris is not added to headless collision geometry. Zero false confirmations here does not measure a live detector's false-positive rate. The world file stays on the simulator/evaluator side. The request only maps the scenario deadline to mission_time_limit_s; it changes no search, navigation or confirmation setting.
Coverage uses the union of both robots' own searched masks on a 0.1 m evaluator grid. Usable floor excludes furniture prisms and a conservative 0.125 m strip beside headless wall segments. The gross figure includes the whole room rectangles. These are sample-weighted house percentages, not an unweighted mean across rooms. The JSON metrics retain per-room counts. Confirmation matching uses the existing headless report's 1.05 m distance threshold. A walk-out requires actual room entry, a final pose outside every room and the complete state.
The first full missions put the origin in the front living room. Family ranch entered 9/9 spaces, searched 98.19% of usable floor and confirmed 5/5 people with zero unmatched confirmation events. Long ranch entered 11/11 spaces, searched 97.31% and confirmed 5/5 with zero unmatched events. One robot in each run held inside on return while air temperature rose. I moved only the fire origin to a rear bedroom, preserving the head start, solver and material parameters, and ran both complete missions again. Initial fire files, scores and command output remain under handoff/houses_evidence with initial_ names. The rear-fire reruns repeated the same return stopping positions, so the origin move did not resolve the failure. I then widened the front entrances and the family living-to-hall opening from 1 m to 1.6 m. Furniture, people, pads and every robot setting stayed identical. The widened-opening long-ranch run still left robot A inside. I then moved its front entrance from the hall end to the living room, with a broad cased opening into the hall. Only living-room furniture was revised; people and the local launch protocol stayed identical. The final results above use the successful widened-opening family run and the final living-entry long run. The west-entry metrics remain in metrics_long_ranch_west_entry.json, and rear-fire metrics remain in metrics_family_ranch_rear_fire.json and metrics_long_ranch_rear_fire.json. The houses were revised after inspecting headless failures. Treat these runs as development checks.
The first launch also caught an off-grid room edge at 5.20 m. I moved it to 5.25 m and added an off-grid rejection check. That failed launch ended before either mission stepped. Its output remains in initial_grid_failure.txt.
The editor files were read twice and compile against the installed Unity managed APIs using tools/check_ranch_editor.py. Existing project collaborators and HDRP use compile-only stand-ins in that check. It does not compile the full Unity project or prove actual scene construction, imported furniture bounds, material appearance, roof shading, oracle export or default-house restoration in the editor. The headless build compiles the existing Brain, sensor types, FloorPlan, FireSim and simulator sources; the logic build also compiles Scenario and its new tests. Atmosphere and RecordingSession changes were source-reviewed. A live Unity build and recording remain untested because the brief forbids launching Unity while it is recording.
the project notes and docs/INTERFACES.md were absent from this worktree because they are ignored. I read their source-checkout copies under /Volumes/Tashon/opencv/smoke-rescue, along with its referenced setup, build-order and simulation documents. I did not write there or change their interfaces. The existing LFS texture differences and untracked handoff brief/log were present at the start and remain outside the proposed commit.
